{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_011capM7ZN5ugAuBJhGdYcBy","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# D020 Task 2: ACDC 14-Edge Selection Method Documentation\n2\t\n3\t## ACDC Cache Format (`cache_acdc.json`)\n4\t\n5\t**Total edges**: 32,491 (all edges in GPT-2 IOI circuit search space)\n6\t\n7\tACDC (Conmy et al., 2023) uses iterative ablation with threshold sweeps. The `auto_circuit` implementation:\n8\t1. Initializes all edge scores to `inf`\n9\t2. For each threshold `tao`, iterates edges top-down by layer\n10\t3. If removing an edge degrades faithfulness (KL divergence) by < `tao`, the edge is pruned and assigned `score = min(tao, current_score)`\n11\t4. Edges that survive ALL thresholds retain `score = inf` — these form the circuit\n12\t\n13\t**Our configuration** (`circuit_discovery.py:118`): `tao_exps=[-2]` with default `tao_bases=[1,3,5,7,9]`\n14\t- Thresholds: 0.01, 0.03, 0.05, 0.07, 0.09\n15\t\n16\t## Score Distribution\n17\t\n18\t| Score | Count | Meaning |\n19\t|-------|-------|---------|\n20\t| inf | 14 | **Retained circuit** — survived all 5 thresholds |\n21\t| 0.09 | 14 | Pruned only at most lenient threshold |\n22\t| 0.07 | 11 | Pruned at tao=0.07 |\n23\t| 0.05 | 50 | Pruned at tao=0.05 |\n24\t| 0.03 | 138 | Pruned at tao=0.03 |\n25\t| 0.01 | 32,264 | Pruned at strictest threshold (most unimportant) |\n26\t\n27\t## 14 Infinity Edges (The Retained Circuit)\n28\t\n29\t```\n30\ta0.h1->a9.h9.v\n31\ta10.h0->logits\n32\ta11.h2->logits\n33\ta5.h5->a8.h6.v\n34\ta7.h9->a9.h9.q\n35\ta8.h6->a9.h6.q\n36\ta8.h6->a9.h9.q\n37\ta9.h6->logits\n38\ta9.h9->logits\n39\tinput->m0\n40\tm0->a10.h0.v\n41\tm0->a5.h5.q\n42\tm0->a9.h6.v\n43\tm0->a9.h9.v\n44\t```\n45\t\n46\t## 14 Score=0.09 Edges (Borderline — Last Pruned)\n47\t\n48\t```\n49\ta10.h1->logits\n50\ta10.h2->logits\n51\ta10.h6->logits\n52\ta10.h7->logits\n53\ta6.h9->a8.h6.v\n54\ta7.h3->a9.h9.q\n55\ta9.h8->logits\n56\tm0->a9.h8.v\n57\tm0->a9.h9.k\n58\tm1->a9.h9.v\n59\tm2->a9.h9.v\n60\tm4->a9.h9.v\n61\tm6->a9.h9.v\n62\tm7->a9.h9.v\n63\t```\n64\t\n65\t## Why `math.isinf()` Is Not Cherry-Picking\n66\t\n67\tThe binarization `{e for e, v in scores.items() if math.isinf(v)}` (`analysis_fix_v3.py:38`) is the **canonical way** to read ACDC output — it is dictated by the algorithm's output format, not a post-hoc cutoff choice:\n68\t\n69\t- ACDC is a **binary** circuit discovery method: an edge is either in the circuit (inf) or pruned (finite)\n70\t- The finite values encode **when** an edge was pruned (which tao step), not a continuous importance score\n71\t- There is no threshold to tune — `isinf` is the only correct reading of the output\n72\t- This is identical to how `auto_circuit` itself interprets ACDC results (line 118 in ACDC source: `(s == t.inf) * 1.0`)\n73\t\n74\tThe 14 score=0.09 edges are a coincidence in count — they are the edges pruned at the **last** (most lenient) threshold, meaning they were the most borderline pruned edges, but still pruned.\n75\t\n76\t## Code Comments Added\n77\t\n78\t- `analysis_fix_v3.py:38-42` — `binarize_acdc` function: explains inf/finite semantics and why isinf is canonical\n79\t- `circuit_discovery.py:111` — `run_acdc` docstring: documents output format\n80\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_017BjPSE6L4fSghmZkphTcJ8","session_id":"ffd92ab4-c587-4272-8b8a-d75b465e4752","uuid":"a371cc80-83b0-4954-b959-4e4147fe2c76","timestamp":"2026-05-13T06:22:21.887Z"}
